IMPORT prelude

-- Judiciary Act 1903 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- what a person charged with a Commonwealth offence meets when the charge is
-- dealt with in a State or Territory court: whether that court can deal with it (s 68(2), (5), (5A), (5B), (5C)); which law
-- governs my arrest, custody, bail and the procedure (s 68(1)); being committed to a higher court for sentence after a guilty
-- plea (s 68(7)-(10)); which court I can be committed to when the Federal Court can also try me (s 68A(2)-(5)); which law of
-- evidence governs my trial (s 79(1), (1A)); and what governs where no written law does (s 80).
--
-- SEAT: the accused person -- someone charged with an offence against a law of the Commonwealth. "I am charged with a
-- Commonwealth offence in the Magistrates Court. Can that court deal with it? Whose rules apply to my bail and my trial --
-- the Commonwealth's or the State's? Which Evidence Act governs?" Sections 68 and 79 are addressed to courts ("shall apply
-- and be applied", "shall ... be binding on all Courts"); they are encoded as regulative rules on the court and as what they
-- mean for the accused. Every finding or satisfaction the Act leaves to the court is asked as an input, never guessed.
--
-- Edition: the compilation registered as C2024C00864 (Compilation No. 51, compilation date 11 December 2024; Federal Register
-- of Legislation, retrieved 13 October 2026), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C1903A00006.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Part I-Part VIII beyond ss 39(2) (as the source of the federal jurisdiction s 68 confers); ss 68(4) (amending
-- an information), 68(6) (arrest at sea), 68(11); ss 68B-68D and 79(1A)(a) (the Federal Court's indictable proceedings under
-- s 68C(5)); ss 69-78B; s 79(2)-(4) (tax recovery suits); s 80A; Parts IXA onwards; civil matters throughout. What the State
-- or Territory law of evidence or procedure says is not encoded (for Western Australia see subjects/au/wa/evidence-act-1906
-- and evidence-act-2025, deposited without an encoding). A charge that is not for a Commonwealth offence, in a court not
-- otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction, answers `not covered`.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the accused's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Judiciary Act 1903 -- the accused person (a Commonwealth offence in a State or Territory court)`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- ss 68(2), 39(2), 79(1): both reach only courts exercising federal jurisdiction
    `my charge is not for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth and the court is not otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction, so ss 68 and 79 do not apply; the State's or Territory's own law governs my case of its own force, and that is not encoded here`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `The States and Territories, and the courts' acts`

-- "The laws of each State or Territory" (s 79(1)). The Territories are named as the Act names them; an external Territory is
-- a Territory.
DECLARE `State or Territory` IS ONE OF
    `Western Australia`
    `New South Wales`
    `Victoria`
    `Queensland`
    `South Australia`
    `Tasmania`
    `the Australian Capital Territory`
    `the Northern Territory`
    `another Territory (such as Norfolk Island or the Jervis Bay Territory)`

-- The stage of a criminal proceeding that s 68(1) and (2) list: "(a) their summary conviction; and (b) their examination and
-- commitment for trial on indictment; and (c) their trial and conviction on indictment; and (d) the hearing and determination
-- of appeals arising out of any such trial or conviction". Bail, arrest and custody are covered at every stage.
DECLARE `Stage` IS ONE OF
    `summary hearing (a charge dealt with summarily, as in a Magistrates Court)`
    `committal (examination and commitment for trial on indictment)`
    `trial on indictment (in a District, County or Supreme Court)`
    `appeal (from such a hearing, trial or conviction)`

-- The parties the regulative rules below name. The accused is the person in the seat; the court is the court exercising the
-- jurisdiction s 68(2) confers, and the committals court is the one s 68A(2) names.
DECLARE `Judiciary party` IS ONE OF
    `the court exercising federal jurisdiction`
    `the committals court`
    `the accused`

DECLARE `Judiciary act` IS ONE OF
    -- s 79(1): applying a State's or Territory's "laws relating to procedure, evidence, and the competency of witnesses"
    `apply the law of evidence of` HAS `whose law` IS A `State or Territory`
    -- s 68(1): applying a State's or Territory's laws "respecting the arrest and custody of offenders ..., and the procedure
    -- for" each stage, "and for holding accused persons to bail"
    `apply the law of arrest, custody, bail and procedure of` HAS `whose law` IS A `State or Territory`
    -- s 68(5A): declining summary jurisdiction over an offence committed in another State or Territory
    `decline to exercise summary jurisdiction`
    -- s 68A(3), (4): making the committal order, with whether the court first invited the Director of Public Prosecutions to
    -- suggest the court of trial or sentence, and whether it considered specifying the court the Director suggested
    `make the committal order` HAS `having first invited the Director to suggest the court` IS A BOOLEAN
                                   `having considered specifying the court the Director suggested` IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `ss 39(2), 68(2) -- is the court exercising federal jurisdiction in my case`

-- s 68(2): the courts of a State or Territory exercising jurisdiction over State or Territory offences "shall, subject to this
-- section and to section 80 of the Constitution, have the like jurisdiction with respect to persons who are charged with
-- offences against the laws of the Commonwealth." That jurisdiction is federal jurisdiction (s 39(2) invests State courts
-- with federal jurisdiction "within the limits of their several jurisdictions"). A court may also be exercising federal
-- jurisdiction for another reason (a matter arising under the Constitution, for example); that is asked as a fact.
GIVEN `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court is exercising federal jurisdiction in my case` IF
    `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` OR `the court is otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction`

§§ `s 68(2), (5), (5C) -- can this State or Territory court deal with my Commonwealth charge (if you have been charged)`

-- s 68(2) gives "the like jurisdiction": the court can deal with my Commonwealth charge at the stages, and within the limits,
-- that it can deal with a State or Territory charge. Locality: s 68(5) confers the summary jurisdiction, and the s 68(7)
-- committal-for-sentence jurisdiction, "notwithstanding any limits as to locality of the jurisdiction of that court under the
-- law of that State or Territory"; s 68(5C) does the same for committal and trial on indictment, but only for "offences
-- committed elsewhere than in a State or Territory (including offences in, over or under any area of the seas that is not
-- part of a State or Territory)". Nothing lifts a locality limit on an appeal. A trial on indictment is "subject to ... section
-- 80 of the Constitution", which says that such a trial "shall be by jury" (the Constitution is not deposited; quoted as s 68
-- points to it).
DECLARE `Can this court deal with my charge` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court has the like jurisdiction over my Commonwealth charge as it has over a State or Territory offence (s 68(2))`
    `yes, and the trial on indictment must be by jury: the court has the like jurisdiction (s 68(2)), subject to section 80 of the Constitution`
    `yes, despite the court's limits as to locality: s 68(5) or (5C) confers the jurisdiction notwithstanding them`
    `no: this court has no jurisdiction at this stage over a like State or Territory offence, so s 68(2) gives it none over my Commonwealth charge`
    `no: the offence is outside the court's limits as to locality, and at this stage s 68(5) and (5C) do not lift them`

@export I am charged with a Commonwealth offence. Can this State or Territory court deal with my charge at this stage?
GIVEN `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are charged with an offence against a law of the Commonwealth (for example under the Criminal Code, the Crimes Act 1914, the Customs Act 1901 or a Commonwealth tax or social security law)
      `the stage` IS A `Stage` @desc Which stage of the case this is: a summary hearing, a committal, a trial on indictment, or an appeal
      `the court has this jurisdiction over a like State or Territory offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this court could deal, at this stage, with a State or Territory offence of the same kind (leaving aside where the offence was committed)
      `the offence is outside the court's limits as to locality` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence was committed outside the area within which this court can deal with State or Territory offences
      `the offence was committed outside every State and Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence was committed outside every State and Territory (for example at sea beyond State waters, or overseas)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can this court deal with my charge`
`Can this court deal with my charge question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence`)
    THEN `not covered` `my charge is not for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth and the court is not otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction, so ss 68 and 79 do not apply; the State's or Territory's own law governs my case of its own force, and that is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF (NOT `the court has this jurisdiction over a like State or Territory offence`)
         THEN `answered` `no: this court has no jurisdiction at this stage over a like State or Territory offence, so s 68(2) gives it none over my Commonwealth charge`
         ELSE IF `the offence is outside the court's limits as to locality`
              THEN IF `the locality limits are lifted at this stage` `the stage` `the offence was committed outside every State and Territory`
                   THEN `answered` `yes, despite the court's limits as to locality: s 68(5) or (5C) confers the jurisdiction notwithstanding them`
                   ELSE `answered` `no: the offence is outside the court's limits as to locality, and at this stage s 68(5) and (5C) do not lift them`
              ELSE IF `the stage` EQUALS `trial on indictment (in a District, County or Supreme Court)`
                   THEN `answered` `yes, and the trial on indictment must be by jury: the court has the like jurisdiction (s 68(2)), subject to section 80 of the Constitution`
                   ELSE `answered` `yes: the court has the like jurisdiction over my Commonwealth charge as it has over a State or Territory offence (s 68(2))`

-- s 68(5)(a): summary conviction, notwithstanding locality; s 68(5C): committal and trial on indictment, for an offence committed
-- elsewhere than in a State or Territory. (s 68(5)(b), committal for sentence under s 68(7), is asked in its own question.)
GIVEN `the stage` IS A `Stage`
      `the offence was committed outside every State and Territory` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the locality limits are lifted at this stage` IF
    `the stage` EQUALS `summary hearing (a charge dealt with summarily, as in a Magistrates Court)`
    OR (    (`the stage` EQUALS `committal (examination and commitment for trial on indictment)` OR `the stage` EQUALS `trial on indictment (in a District, County or Supreme Court)`)
        AND `the offence was committed outside every State and Territory`)

§§ `s 68(5A), (5B) -- if the offence was committed in another State or Territory`

-- s 68(5A): "A court of a State on which jurisdiction in relation to the summary conviction of persons charged with offences
-- against the laws of the Commonwealth is conferred by subsection (2) may, where it is satisfied that it is appropriate to do
-- so, having regard to all the circumstances, including the public interest, decline to exercise that jurisdiction in
-- relation to an offence against a law of the Commonwealth committed in another State." (5B): "State includes Territory."
-- A permission to the court: as a regulative rule, the court MAY decline; for me, whether it can.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Judiciary party` `Judiciary act`
DECIDE `s 68(5A) -- the court may decline summary jurisdiction over an offence committed in another State or Territory` IS
    PARTY `the court exercising federal jurisdiction`
    MAY   `decline to exercise summary jurisdiction`
    HENCE FULFILLED

DECLARE `Can the court refuse to deal with my charge summarily` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court may decline to exercise its summary jurisdiction over an offence committed in another State or Territory, and it is satisfied that declining is appropriate (s 68(5A))`
    `no: the court is not satisfied that declining is appropriate, so it deals with my charge (s 68(5A))`
    `no: s 68(5A) reaches only a Commonwealth offence committed in another State or Territory, dealt with summarily`

@export The offence I am charged with happened in another State or Territory. Can the court here refuse to deal with it summarily?
GIVEN `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are charged with an offence against a law of the Commonwealth
      `the charge is being dealt with summarily` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the charge is being dealt with summarily (for example in a Magistrates Court), rather than at a committal or trial on indictment
      `the offence was committed in another State or Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence is said to have been committed in a State or Territory other than the one where this court sits
      `the court is satisfied that declining is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied, having regard to all the circumstances including the public interest, that it is appropriate to decline to deal with the charge
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can the court refuse to deal with my charge summarily`
`Can the court refuse to deal with my charge summarily question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence`)
    THEN `not covered` `my charge is not for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth and the court is not otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction, so ss 68 and 79 do not apply; the State's or Territory's own law governs my case of its own force, and that is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`the charge is being dealt with summarily` AND `the offence was committed in another State or Territory`))
         THEN `answered` `no: s 68(5A) reaches only a Commonwealth offence committed in another State or Territory, dealt with summarily`
         ELSE IF `the court is satisfied that declining is appropriate`
              THEN `answered` `yes: the court may decline to exercise its summary jurisdiction over an offence committed in another State or Territory, and it is satisfied that declining is appropriate (s 68(5A))`
              ELSE `answered` `no: the court is not satisfied that declining is appropriate, so it deals with my charge (s 68(5A))`

§§ `s 68(1) -- whose law governs my arrest, custody, bail and the procedure in my case`

-- s 68(1): "The laws of a State or Territory respecting the arrest and custody of offenders or persons charged with offences,
-- and the procedure for: (a) their summary conviction; and (b) their examination and commitment for trial on indictment; and
-- (c) their trial and conviction on indictment; and (d) the hearing and determination of appeals ...; and for holding accused
-- persons to bail, shall, subject to this section, apply and be applied so far as they are applicable to persons who are
-- charged with offences against the laws of the Commonwealth in respect of whom jurisdiction is conferred on the several
-- courts of that State or Territory by this section." So the law is that of the State or Territory whose court has the
-- jurisdiction. Where a Commonwealth law makes its own provision on a point (s 68(11) keeps "any power of arrest conferred by
-- ... any other law"), that law governs the point; whether one does is asked as a fact.
--
-- As a regulative rule: the court MUST NOT apply another State's or Territory's law of arrest, custody, bail or procedure.
-- (Without a deadline, the rule is kept by not breaking it; a trace shows it breached when the wrong law is applied.)
GIVEN `the State or Territory whose court has the jurisdiction` IS A `State or Territory`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Judiciary party` `Judiciary act`
DECIDE `s 68(1) -- the court must apply that State's or Territory's law of arrest, custody, bail and procedure` IS
    PARTY `the court exercising federal jurisdiction`
    SHANT `apply the law of arrest, custody, bail and procedure of` other PROVIDED (NOT (other EQUALS `the State or Territory whose court has the jurisdiction`))
    LEST  BREACH BY `the court exercising federal jurisdiction` BECAUSE "s 68(1): the laws of the State or Territory whose court has the jurisdiction apply to a person charged with a Commonwealth offence"

DECLARE `Whose procedure governs` IS ONE OF
    `the law of that State or Territory on arrest, custody, bail and procedure, so far as it is applicable (s 68(1))` HAS `the State or Territory` IS A `State or Territory`
    `the Commonwealth law that makes its own provision on the point; the law of that State or Territory governs the rest, so far as it is applicable (s 68(1))` HAS `the State or Territory` IS A `State or Territory`

@export I am charged with a Commonwealth offence. Whose law governs my arrest, custody, bail and the procedure in my case: the Commonwealth's or the State's?
GIVEN `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are charged with an offence against a law of the Commonwealth
      `the State or Territory whose court has the jurisdiction` IS A `State or Territory` @desc The State or Territory whose court is dealing with the charge
      `a Commonwealth law makes its own provision on the point` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a law of the Commonwealth makes its own provision on the particular point (for example a Commonwealth power of arrest, or a Commonwealth rule about bail for that offence); answer FALSE if there is none
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Whose procedure governs`
`Whose procedure governs question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence`)
    THEN `not covered` `my charge is not for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth and the court is not otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction, so ss 68 and 79 do not apply; the State's or Territory's own law governs my case of its own force, and that is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF `a Commonwealth law makes its own provision on the point`
         THEN `answered` (`the Commonwealth law that makes its own provision on the point; the law of that State or Territory governs the rest, so far as it is applicable (s 68(1))` `the State or Territory whose court has the jurisdiction`)
         ELSE `answered` (`the law of that State or Territory on arrest, custody, bail and procedure, so far as it is applicable (s 68(1))` `the State or Territory whose court has the jurisdiction`)

§§ `s 79(1), (1A) -- which law of evidence governs my trial`

-- s 79(1): "The laws of each State or Territory, including the laws relating to procedure, evidence, and the competency of
-- witnesses, shall, except as otherwise provided by the Constitution or the laws of the Commonwealth, be binding on all Courts
-- exercising federal jurisdiction in that State or Territory in all cases to which they are applicable."
-- s 79(1A): "a Court exercising federal jurisdiction in a proceeding is taken to be exercising federal jurisdiction in: (a) if
-- the laws of a State or Territory apply to a part of the proceeding because of subsection 68C(5)--that State or Territory; or
-- (b) if paragraph (a) does not apply: (i) if the proceeding is transferred or remitted--the State or Territory to which the
-- proceeding is transferred or remitted; and (ii) otherwise--the State or Territory in which the proceeding is commenced".
-- Paragraph (a) concerns indictable proceedings in the Federal Court (s 68C) and is not encoded.
GIVEN `the State or Territory where the proceeding was commenced` IS A `State or Territory`
      `the proceeding was transferred or remitted` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the State or Territory it was transferred or remitted to` IS A `State or Territory`
GIVETH A `State or Territory`
DECIDE `the State or Territory in which the court exercises federal jurisdiction` IS
    IF `the proceeding was transferred or remitted`
    THEN `the State or Territory it was transferred or remitted to`
    ELSE `the State or Territory where the proceeding was commenced`

-- The same duty, as a regulative rule: a court exercising federal jurisdiction in a State or Territory MUST NOT apply another
-- State's or Territory's law of evidence. Where a Commonwealth law "otherwise provides" on a point, that law, not the State
-- law, governs the point; the rule is about the State or Territory law that s 79 picks up.
GIVEN `the State or Territory whose law binds the court` IS A `State or Territory`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Judiciary party` `Judiciary act`
DECIDE `s 79(1) -- the court must apply the law of evidence of the State or Territory in which it exercises federal jurisdiction` IS
    PARTY `the court exercising federal jurisdiction`
    SHANT `apply the law of evidence of` other PROVIDED (NOT (other EQUALS `the State or Territory whose law binds the court`))
    LEST  BREACH BY `the court exercising federal jurisdiction` BECAUSE "s 79(1): the laws of evidence of the State or Territory in which the court exercises federal jurisdiction bind it"

-- What it means for me. In a federal court, the Evidence Act 1995 applies by its own s 4(1) ("all proceedings in a federal
-- court"), which is a law of the Commonwealth that "otherwise provides". In a State or Territory court, the State's or
-- Territory's law of evidence binds the court, except where the Constitution or a Commonwealth law otherwise provides on the
-- point -- for example the provisions of the Evidence Act 1995 that reach other courts by their own terms (its s 4 Note 3 and
-- Note 4 point to s 5, ss 185-187, and this s 79; s 131B extends Division 1C and s 131A to every proceeding for a Commonwealth
-- offence; s 128(12)-(14) gives a State certificate effect in a Commonwealth prosecution). Whether one does is asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Which evidence law governs` IS ONE OF
    `the law of evidence of that State or Territory, which s 79(1) makes binding on the court` HAS `the State or Territory` IS A `State or Territory`
    `on this point, the Commonwealth law that otherwise provides (s 79(1)); on everything else, the law of evidence of that State or Territory` HAS `the State or Territory` IS A `State or Territory`
    `the Evidence Act 1995: it applies to all proceedings in a federal court by its own s 4(1), a law of the Commonwealth that otherwise provides (s 79(1))`

@export I am charged with a Commonwealth offence. Which law of evidence governs my trial: the Commonwealth Evidence Act 1995, or the evidence law of the State or Territory?
GIVEN `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are charged with an offence against a law of the Commonwealth
      `the court is otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, apart from the charge, the court is exercising federal jurisdiction in your case (for example because a matter arising under the Constitution has been raised); answer FALSE if unsure
      `my case is in a federal court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your case is in the High Court or another court created by the federal Parliament (for example the Federal Court of Australia), rather than in a State or Territory court (a Magistrates or Local Court, a District or County Court, or a State or Territory Supreme Court)
      `the State or Territory where the proceeding was commenced` IS A `State or Territory` @desc The State or Territory in which your case was started
      `the proceeding was transferred or remitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your case has been transferred or remitted to a court in another State or Territory
      `the State or Territory it was transferred or remitted to` IS A `State or Territory` @desc If it was transferred or remitted, the State or Territory it went to (otherwise, the same as where it was started)
      `a Commonwealth law otherwise provides on this point of evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, on the particular point of evidence, the Constitution or a law of the Commonwealth makes its own provision for a court like this one (for example a provision of the Evidence Act 1995 that applies in all Australian courts, such as ss 5, 131B, 128(12)-(14) or 185-187); answer FALSE if there is none
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Which evidence law governs`
`Which evidence law governs question` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`the court is exercising federal jurisdiction in my case` `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` `the court is otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction`))
    THEN `not covered` `my charge is not for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth and the court is not otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction, so ss 68 and 79 do not apply; the State's or Territory's own law governs my case of its own force, and that is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF `my case is in a federal court`
         THEN `answered` `the Evidence Act 1995: it applies to all proceedings in a federal court by its own s 4(1), a law of the Commonwealth that otherwise provides (s 79(1))`
         ELSE IF `a Commonwealth law otherwise provides on this point of evidence`
              THEN `answered` (`on this point, the Commonwealth law that otherwise provides (s 79(1)); on everything else, the law of evidence of that State or Territory` (`the State or Territory in which the court exercises federal jurisdiction` `the State or Territory where the proceeding was commenced` `the proceeding was transferred or remitted` `the State or Territory it was transferred or remitted to`))
              ELSE `answered` (`the law of evidence of that State or Territory, which s 79(1) makes binding on the court` (`the State or Territory in which the court exercises federal jurisdiction` `the State or Territory where the proceeding was commenced` `the proceeding was transferred or remitted` `the State or Territory it was transferred or remitted to`))

-- s 79(1) also names "the competency of witnesses": who may and must give evidence is a matter for the same State or
-- Territory law. Asked separately because a witness's competence is a question the accused asks on its own.
@export Whose law decides who can be a witness at my trial, and whether I can be made to give evidence?
GIVEN `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are charged with an offence against a law of the Commonwealth
      `my case is in a federal court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your case is in the High Court or another court created by the federal Parliament, rather than in a State or Territory court
      `the State or Territory where the proceeding was commenced` IS A `State or Territory` @desc The State or Territory in which your case was started
      `the proceeding was transferred or remitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your case has been transferred or remitted to a court in another State or Territory
      `the State or Territory it was transferred or remitted to` IS A `State or Territory` @desc If it was transferred or remitted, the State or Territory it went to (otherwise, the same as where it was started)
      `a Commonwealth law otherwise provides on this point of evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a law of the Commonwealth makes its own provision on the competence or compellability of this witness for a court like this one; answer FALSE if there is none
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Which evidence law governs`
`Whose law decides who can be a witness question` MEANS
    `Which evidence law governs question` `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` FALSE `my case is in a federal court` `the State or Territory where the proceeding was commenced` `the proceeding was transferred or remitted` `the State or Territory it was transferred or remitted to` `a Commonwealth law otherwise provides on this point of evidence`

§§ `s 80 -- if no written law covers a point in my case`

-- s 80: "So far as the laws of the Commonwealth are not applicable or so far as their provisions are insufficient to carry
-- them into effect, or to provide adequate remedies or punishment, the common law in Australia as modified by the
-- Constitution and by the statute law in force in the State or Territory in which the Court in which the jurisdiction is
-- exercised is held shall, so far as it is applicable and not inconsistent with the Constitution and the laws of the
-- Commonwealth, govern all Courts exercising federal jurisdiction in the exercise of their jurisdiction in civil and
-- criminal matters."
DECLARE `What fills the gap` IS ONE OF
    `the laws of the Commonwealth on that point, which are applicable and sufficient (s 80 does not reach it)`
    `the common law in Australia, as modified by the Constitution and by the statute law in force in the State or Territory where the court is held, so far as applicable and not inconsistent with the Constitution and Commonwealth law (s 80)` HAS `the State or Territory` IS A `State or Territory`

@export If the Commonwealth law does not deal with a point in my case, what governs it?
GIVEN `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are charged with an offence against a law of the Commonwealth
      `the Commonwealth laws are applicable and sufficient on the point` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the laws of the Commonwealth apply to the point and are enough to carry them into effect and to provide adequate remedies or punishment
      `the State or Territory where the court is held` IS A `State or Territory` @desc The State or Territory where the court dealing with your case sits
GIVETH AN `Answer` `What fills the gap`
`What fills the gap question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence`)
    THEN `not covered` `my charge is not for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth and the court is not otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction, so ss 68 and 79 do not apply; the State's or Territory's own law governs my case of its own force, and that is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF `the Commonwealth laws are applicable and sufficient on the point`
         THEN `answered` `the laws of the Commonwealth on that point, which are applicable and sufficient (s 80 does not reach it)`
         ELSE `answered` (`the common law in Australia, as modified by the Constitution and by the statute law in force in the State or Territory where the court is held, so far as applicable and not inconsistent with the Constitution and Commonwealth law (s 80)` `the State or Territory where the court is held`)

§§ `s 68(7)-(10) -- if I plead guilty in a summary court to a charge that could go on indictment`

-- s 68(7): the procedure and jurisdiction in s 68(1) and (2) "shall be deemed to include procedure and jurisdiction in
-- accordance with provisions of a law of a State or Territory under which a person who, in proceedings before a court of
-- summary jurisdiction, pleads guilty to a charge for which he or she could be prosecuted on indictment may be committed to a
-- court having jurisdiction to try offences on indictment to be sentenced or otherwise dealt with without being tried in that
-- court". (8): that holds "notwithstanding that, apart from this section, the offence would be required to be prosecuted on
-- indictment". (9): the State law's "indictable offences" include "an offence against a law of the Commonwealth that may be
-- prosecuted on indictment". (10): the person "shall, for the purpose of ascertaining the sentence that may be imposed, be
-- deemed to have been prosecuted and convicted on indictment in that court". Whether the State or Territory has such a law is
-- asked as a fact (it is not encoded). s 68(5)(b) lifts the court's limits as to locality for this procedure.
DECLARE `Can I be sent to a higher court for sentence` IS ONE OF
    `yes: under the State or Territory procedure s 68(7) picks up, I may be committed to a court that tries offences on indictment to be sentenced without a trial there, and that court sentences me as if I had been prosecuted and convicted on indictment in it (s 68(7), (10))`
    `no: my charge could not be prosecuted on indictment, so s 68(7) does not apply`
    `no: the State or Territory has no law for committal for sentence after a guilty plea in a summary court, so s 68(7) has nothing to pick up`
    `no: I have not pleaded guilty in a court of summary jurisdiction`

@export I pleaded guilty in a summary court (such as a Magistrates Court) to a Commonwealth charge that could go on indictment. Can I be sent to a higher court for sentence, and on what basis?
GIVEN `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are charged with an offence against a law of the Commonwealth
      `I pleaded guilty before a court of summary jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you pleaded guilty to the charge before a court of summary jurisdiction (such as a Magistrates or Local Court)
      `the charge may be prosecuted on indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commonwealth offence may be prosecuted on indictment (including one that must be)
      `the State or Territory has a committal for sentence procedure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law of the State or Territory lets a court of summary jurisdiction commit a person who pleads guilty to an indictable charge to a higher court to be sentenced
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I be sent to a higher court for sentence`
`Can I be sent to a higher court for sentence question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence`)
    THEN `not covered` `my charge is not for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth and the court is not otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction, so ss 68 and 79 do not apply; the State's or Territory's own law governs my case of its own force, and that is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF (NOT `I pleaded guilty before a court of summary jurisdiction`)
         THEN `answered` `no: I have not pleaded guilty in a court of summary jurisdiction`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the charge may be prosecuted on indictment`)
              THEN `answered` `no: my charge could not be prosecuted on indictment, so s 68(7) does not apply`
              ELSE IF (NOT `the State or Territory has a committal for sentence procedure`)
                   THEN `answered` `no: the State or Territory has no law for committal for sentence after a guilty plea in a summary court, so s 68(7) has nothing to pick up`
                   ELSE `answered` `yes: under the State or Territory procedure s 68(7) picks up, I may be committed to a court that tries offences on indictment to be sentenced without a trial there, and that court sentences me as if I had been prosecuted and convicted on indictment in it (s 68(7), (10))`

§§ `s 68A -- if I am committed for trial or sentence and the Federal Court could also try me`

-- s 68A(1): applies "if both: (a) the Federal Court of Australia; and (b) a court of a State or Territory (the superior State or
-- Territory court); have jurisdiction to try a person on indictment for an indictable offence against a law of the
-- Commonwealth". (2): the committals court "may ... (a) commit the person for trial on indictment for the offence before
-- either: (i) the Federal Court of Australia; or (ii) the superior State or Territory court; or (b) if the person pleads guilty
-- to the offence, commit the person for sentencing" before either. (3): before making the committal order, it "must invite the
-- Director of Public Prosecutions to suggest the court before which the person is to be tried or sentenced"; (4): "When making
-- the committal order, the ... committals court must consider specifying the court suggested by the Director". (5): if the
-- order specifies the Federal Court, the court's bail power extends to bail to appear before the Federal Court.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Judiciary party` `Judiciary act`
DECIDE `s 68A(3), (4) -- the committals court must invite the Director's suggestion and consider it before committing` IS
    PARTY `the committals court`
    SHANT `make the committal order` invited considered PROVIDED (NOT (invited AND considered))
    LEST  BREACH BY `the committals court` BECAUSE "s 68A(3), (4): the committals court must invite the Director of Public Prosecutions to suggest the court, and consider specifying it, when making the committal order"

DECLARE `Which court can I be committed to` IS ONE OF
    `either the Federal Court of Australia or the superior State or Territory court: the committals court chooses, after inviting the Director of Public Prosecutions to suggest one and considering specifying it (s 68A(2)-(4)); if it chooses the Federal Court, it may grant me bail to appear there (s 68A(5))`
    `the superior State or Territory court: the Federal Court has no jurisdiction to try me on indictment for this offence, so s 68A does not apply (s 68(2))`
    `no committal: s 68A applies only to an indictable Commonwealth offence, at a committal for trial or, on a guilty plea, for sentence`

@export I am being committed for trial (or, on a guilty plea, for sentence) on an indictable Commonwealth offence. Which court can I be sent to?
GIVEN `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are charged with an offence against a law of the Commonwealth
      `the offence is indictable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence can be tried on indictment (before a judge and jury)
      `the committals court proposes to commit me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the end of the committal hearing, the court proposes to commit you for trial on indictment or, because you pleaded guilty, for sentence
      `the Federal Court can also try me on indictment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Federal Court of Australia also has jurisdiction to try you on indictment for this offence (it has for some offences, such as criminal cartel offences)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Which court can I be committed to`
`Which court can I be committed to question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I am charged with a Commonwealth offence`)
    THEN `not covered` `my charge is not for an offence against a law of the Commonwealth and the court is not otherwise exercising federal jurisdiction, so ss 68 and 79 do not apply; the State's or Territory's own law governs my case of its own force, and that is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`the offence is indictable` AND `the committals court proposes to commit me`))
         THEN `answered` `no committal: s 68A applies only to an indictable Commonwealth offence, at a committal for trial or, on a guilty plea, for sentence`
         ELSE IF `the Federal Court can also try me on indictment`
              THEN `answered` `either the Federal Court of Australia or the superior State or Territory court: the committals court chooses, after inviting the Director of Public Prosecutions to suggest one and considering specifying it (s 68A(2)-(4)); if it chooses the Federal Court, it may grant me bail to appear there (s 68A(5))`
              ELSE `answered` `the superior State or Territory court: the Federal Court has no jurisdiction to try me on indictment for this offence, so s 68A does not apply (s 68(2))`
